# Coding Skills Workflow

For every coding task (implementation, refactoring, bug fix, or test work), read and follow these skills before writing code:

- `~/.cursor/skills/design-patterns/SKILL.md`
- `~/.cursor/skills/atdd-java/SKILL.md`

Read the full skill file when the task starts. Do not rely on memory alone.

## Design Patterns

Use the design-patterns skill when:

- Choosing structure for new components or refactors
- Evaluating whether a GoF or enterprise pattern fits
- Reviewing abstraction boundaries

Rules:

- Prefer the simplest solution; do not force patterns
- If no pattern adds value, say so and implement plainly
- Use Java 21 idioms, composition over inheritance, constructor injection

## ATDD (Java)

Use the atdd-java skill when:

- Implementing or changing user-facing behavior
- Adding or updating tests for features, APIs, or domain logic
- The project already uses Cucumber/Gherkin, or acceptance tests are appropriate

Workflow:

1. Write or update a failing `.feature` scenario first when practical
2. Implement minimal step definitions and production code (red → green)
3. Refactor only after tests pass

If Cucumber is not present and the change is trivial, use the project's existing test style — but still follow given/when/then structure from the skill.

## Task Start Checklist

- [ ] Read design-patterns skill for structural decisions
- [ ] Read atdd-java skill when behavior or tests are in scope
- [ ] Match existing project conventions over skill defaults when they conflict
